{"authored" [{"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "425d5a71c4ad064b44f858c7994519e7f6c779771f166c169d79f678eba9b755" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114271" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/state.json" "file_sha256" "2c5bb4153b88cc3d3b42549884972e3db986d4569618d9cc19ca0c2c80185c58" "basis" 1225 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0),$ and $(2,4).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,),\\,(\\,-2,0\\,),\\,(\\,2,4\\,).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9dad6845bf9eca2532d48ba52f667b28cf0c8fd38509d319edf3111cbe85818" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-343146" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a6425d41110879f4a29a2f7003045591859c38c7b286fa58e5e45c6096a91e7" "basis" 2277 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-22826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/state.json" "file_sha256" "cdd325eaa31692585c85cbddf5de959b04a07a04114ab931cea929606a1919a1"} {"question" "q-22826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/state.json" "file_sha256" "cdd325eaa31692585c85cbddf5de959b04a07a04114ab931cea929606a1919a1"} {"question" "q-22826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2db83435a202e9a7402f51105f6cc50d6e14cc9cac25e9e4c9b257a70d5e9c47"} {"question" "q-22826" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/state.json" "file_sha256" "cdd325eaa31692585c85cbddf5de959b04a07a04114ab931cea929606a1919a1"} {"question" "q-22826" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/state.json" "file_sha256" "cdd325eaa31692585c85cbddf5de959b04a07a04114ab931cea929606a1919a1"} {"question" "q-22826" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_gra